    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d152">

                <pb facs="0304" n="276" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v23p2/pg276"/>

                <head>152. Circular Telegram From the Department of State to Certain Diplomatic
                    Missions and Consular Offices<anchor n="1" xml:id="d152-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1956-06-04T12:55:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 4,
                                1956—12:55 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>845. Tokyo also pass CINCUNC. Geneva for Johnson. Stockholm’s 1125.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d152-f2"/> Embassies Bern and Stockholm should confirm to
                    Governments intention UN Command continue reporting entry and departure south
                    Korea personnel and combat equipment to MAC and NNSC.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d152-f3"/></p>
                <pb facs="0305" n="277" xml:id="pg277"/>
                <p>Sixteen informed May 4 meeting NNSC would continue receive UN Command reports in
                    DZ and if question raised other Embassies may confirm this as U.S. position.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Hoover</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d152-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d152-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 795.00/6–456. Secret. Drafted by Nes;
                    cleared with L, UNP, EUR, and Defense; and approved by Murphy. Sent to Addis
                    Ababa, Ankara, Athens, Bern, Bogota, Brussels, Canberra, The Hague, London,
                    Luxembourg, Manila, Ottawa, Paris, Seoul, Stockholm, Taipei, Wellington, Tokyo,
                    Hong Kong, Geneva, and USUN.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d152-f2" n="2" xml:id="d152-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 1125 from Stockholm, Ambassador Cabot reported that he had informed
                    the Swedish Foreign Minister about the UNC decision to remove the NNITs to the
                    demilitarized zone. The Foreign Minister seemed “almost relieved” and asked if
                    the NNSC would continue to receive reports in the demilitarized zone. (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ibid.,</hi> 795.00/5– 2956)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d152-f3" n="3" xml:id="d152-f3-content">
                <p>In a memorandum to Murphy on June 1, Robertson posed the question of whether the
                    U.N. Command should continue to make reports to the NNSC in the demilitarized
                    zone. Robertson proposed a meeting on June 4 to discuss the question. A marginal
                    notation in Hemmendinger’s hand on a copy of the memorandum indicates that
                    Murphy, Robertson, Phleger, Bond, and Hemmendinger were the principal
                    participants at the meeting held on June 4, and that it was agreed at the
                    meeting that reporting should continue, and that new equipment should be
                    introduced into South Korea as a matter of “interpretation”. (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ibid.,</hi> NA Files: Lot 59 D 407, Memos and Mem-cons
                    NNSC 1956)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
